ACTION: REVISE 2
TITLE: Relaxed Equivariance for Metanetworks via Soft Constraints (Improved)
PROPOSAL: Refine the previous proposal by incorporating a dynamically adjusted compromise between the strict invariance requirement and expressiveness of the metanetwork. This is achieved by introducing an adaptive tuning mechanism that adjusts the scalar hyperparameter of the soft constraint term during the training process based on the statistics of the metanetwork's embedding space, leveraging recent advancements in adaptive soft-margin learning in neural networks.
CONTROLS: Experimental comparison against vanilla metanetworks and strict-equivariance enforcing metanetworks on benchmark datasets for implicit neural representation classification and editing;
Conduct a detailed ablation study to evaluate the effect of the adaptive tuning mechanism, the incorporation of soft constraints and its trade-off with expressivity;
Additionally, perform a sensitivity analysis on the selection of the initial and final bounds of the adaptive tuning mechanism;
COST: ~70 GPU-hours
PREDICTION: Expected result: The revised relaxed equivariant metanetwork can better balance invariance and expressivity, achieving higher accuracy on real-world benchmarks while maintaining robustness. Where it could fail: Difficulty in determining the adaptive bounds for optimal performance across diverse task configurations and dataset statistics.

ACTION: REVISE 1
TITLE: Characterizing Strict Equivariance for Metanetworks (Conditional Generalizability)
PROPOSAL: Refine the initial proposal on strict equivariance by deriving a theoretical framework that establishes conditions for the generalizability of metanetworks to unseen neural network architectures and weights, assuming they satisfy strict equivariance. This should provide insights into the scope of applicability and limits of strict-equivariance enforcing metanetworks.
CONTROLS: Analyze the theoretical implications of the framework on the generalizability of strict-equivariance enforcing metanetworks;
Develop a set of necessary and sufficient conditions for the generalizability of strict-equivariance enforcing metanetworks to novel architectures and weights;
Provide a detailed comparison with relaxed equivariant metanetworks and other state-of-the-art architectures in terms of generalizability;
COST: ~40 GPU-hours
PREDICTION: Expected result: Characterizing strict equivariance for metanetworks under conditional generalizability will establish the scope of applicability and uncover potential limitations of strict-equivariance enforcing metanetworks. Where it could fail: Difficulty in deriving sufficient conditions for generalizability and potential incompatibility of strict equivariance with the complexities of real-world neural network architectures.

ACTION: ADD
TITLE: Efficient Metanetwork Design via Symmetry Avoidance
PROPOSAL: Investigate a novel approach to designing efficient metanetworks that avoid explicit consideration of symmetry groups, instead focusing on learning to represent weights in a manner that is invariant to symmetry-preserving transformations, while simultaneously leveraging recent advances in the realizability of neural network architectures through symmetry-aware design.
CONTROLS: Compare against state-of-the-art metanetwork architectures on benchmark datasets for neural network generalization prediction from weights;
Examine the role of symmetry awareness in efficiently designing metanetworks for different neural network architectures and weight spaces;
Also, analyze the properties of metanetworks trained with symmetry avoidance in terms of their resilience to over-smoothing, sensitivity to hyperparameters, and expressiveness;
COST: ~80 GPU-hours
PREDICTION: Expected result: Symmetry-avoidance metanetworks will achieve improved performance on benchmark tasks and demonstrate efficient design for various neural network architectures, at the cost of potentially increased training time and complexity. Where it could fail: Difficulty in establishing a clear understanding of the relations between symmetry awareness and metanetwork performance.

ACTION: REVISE 5
TITLE: Efficient Metanetwork Design via Symmetry-Aware Gradient Sampling
PROPOSAL: Refine the previous proposal on efficient metanetwork design by incorporating symmetry-aware gradient sampling to effectively explore the space of feasible symmetry-preserving transformations, thereby reducing the computational cost of learning symmetry-avoiding metanetworks and enhancing their expressiveness and performance.
CONTROLS: Compare against state-of-the-art metanetwork architectures on benchmark datasets for neural network generalization prediction from weights;
Analyze the impact of symmetry-aware gradient sampling on the convergence rate, expressiveness, and resilience to over-smoothing of metanetworks;
Also, examine the trade-off between the efficiency of symmetry-aware gradient sampling and the accuracy of symmetry-avoidance metanetworks;
COST: ~100 GPU-hours
PREDICTION: Expected result: Efficient metanetwork design via symmetry-aware gradient sampling will significantly improve the performance and convergence rate of metanetworks by effectively navigating the space of feasible symmetry-preserving transformations. Where it could fail: Difficulty in establishing a clear understanding of the trade-offs between the efficiency of sampling and the expressiveness of symmetry-aware metanetworks.

ACTION: ADD
TITLE: Unifying Equivariant Metanetworks for Neural Network Analysis
PROPOSAL: Develop a unified framework for equivariant metanetworks, integrating the insights and techniques developed in previous proposals to create a comprehensive structure for analyzing neural networks, learning from their weights and coalescing multiple views of a network into a single interpretable representation.
CONTROLS: Examine the performance and versatility of the unified framework on a wide range of neural network architectures, including convolutional networks, recurrent neural networks, and multi-layer perceptrons, across different tasks and datasets;
Analyze the scalability and efficiency of the unified framework on large-scale neural networks and real-world problems;
Also, expand and generalize the unified framework to include additional neural network types, architectures, and tasks to further enhance its universality;
COST: ~200 GPU-hours
PREDICTION: Expected result: Unifying equivariant metanetworks will provide a powerful, versatile, and interpretable framework for analyzing neural networks, effectively capturing their structure and behavior. Where it could fail: Difficulty in balancing the level of abstraction and expressiveness with computational efficiency and scalability.
